  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold low-level thicknesses and strong high pressure \nover the region will support frost/freeze impacts across much of ENC \nlate tonight into Sunday morning. Additional impacts are possible \nearly next week as well, although a killing freeze tonight would end \nthe frost/freeze program for the season where it occurs, and \nadditional frost/freeze headlines may not be needed anymore. The \nFreeze Warning in effect for tonight still looks good. The only \nchange was to add a Frost Advisory for the counties surrounding the \nwarning where winds should decouple, especially away from bodies of \nwater. It should be noted that for both headlines, the greatest risk \nof frost/freeze impacts will be away from bodies of water, with some \nimmediate coastal/beach communities probably not seeing any \nfrost/freeze impacts. The risk of frost impacts may be lessened \nSunday night as a weak cold front moves through which will keep the \nboundary layer a bit more mixed. High pressure then reasserts itself \nMonday and Tuesday night, with frost potential returning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned very dry airmass and low TD's \neach day over the coming week will exacerbate the worsening drought \nconditions in place across ENC, and fortunately winds are not \nexpected to be strong with the high nearby. However, fire weather \nconcerns may grow with the curing vegetation combined with low RH \nvalues each afternoon.",
